Model of black hole formation without an internal singularity

Consider a typical black hole with the following parameters: diameter 15 km, rotation speed 1000 revolutions per second, equatorial rotation speed 47,000 km/s. This is a stable Kerr black hole.
Formation mechanism:
During stellar collapse, the initial angular momentum, which is never zero, increases in proportion to the star's contraction.
Stellar collapse occurs when the fundamental form-generating motion in the Universe manifests itself. This motion is ROTATION.
The collapse results in a primarily compressed object whose equatorial rotation speed approaches the speed of light. This is the PRIMARY phase of “super giant” compression into a black hole, up to the moment of maximum density. During this compression phase, the compressed matter assumes a spherical shape filled with material. Based on reverse modeling involving centrifugal forces, its rotation speed can be estimated at several tens of kilohertz. This rotation frequency and the acceleration toward the event horizon cause the matter to evacuate the black hole's center, and the rotation frequency drops to approximately 1000 revolutions per second.


The black hole stabilizes at this frequency and now represents an EMPTY sphere, with all of its matter located beneath the event horizon as a thick shell. Below this shell, all the way to the center, there is neither matter, nor space, nor time, nor any coordinate systems. Space has been expelled by compression (no distance remains between the compressed nuclei). Time and reference frames cannot exist independently of space, and thus they are also absent inside the black hole.
The space-free region inside the black hole possesses the following properties:
    • absolutely black (non-reflective)
    • absolutely opaque (no medium for light propagation)
    • absolutely rigid (nothing inside can be described geometrically and does not exist without time)
    • has no mass
    • has no inertia
    • has no thermal conductivity
    • generates no friction (no counter-surface for friction to arise) and so forth; that is, it lacks all attributes of a material object. 


A region devoid of space, time, and coordinate systems cannot be called space, because space is absent there.
This model resolves the problems listed in the table. Furthermore, the absence of a conductive medium inside prevents light from penetrating inward, and the speed of light limit cannot be violated where a singularity was previously assumed.



The connection between the speed of light and the Law of Conservation of Energy

The fundamentality of the speed of light is based on the Law of Conservation of Energy. This law acts as a natural constraint on the speed of light. Explanation: To impart energy to a particle for superluminal motion, the energy transfer rate in the direction of motion would also need to exceed the speed of light. This renders superluminal acceleration technology impossible. It is also impossible to endow a particle with its own energy reserve for superluminal motion, as no particle can contain infinite energy.
The "empty black hole" construction principle applies to all similar forms of matter organization, down to and including elementary particles.
Rotation is the primary form-generating factor.
When a black hole absorbs external energy, it adjusts its dimensions. When the equatorial velocity reaches the speed of light limit, the only remaining regulation mechanism is an increase in diameter. According to the principle of centrifugal forces, increasing the diameter reduces the rotation frequency and the tangential equatorial velocity. This natural feedback mechanism ensures the protection of the fundamental principle of the speed of light limit.

Merger of two black holes (detection of gravitational waves with a single peak)

As black holes approach each other, they orbit a common center but cannot make contact with their diameters. Gravity precedes the collision, and the more massive black hole (the predator) strips matter from the surface of the victim black hole.
On the gravitational-wave profile accompanying the black hole merger, noise from the matter transfer from the "victim" to the "predator" increases.
The event horizon of the victim black hole ruptures, exposing the region without coordinate systems. External space immediately fills this region (nature abhors a vacuum), causing a hydrodynamic impact of space against itself. This impact contributes its own energy to the black hole decay process and explains the energy defect of the decay calculated based solely on the black hole's mass. This impact generates a gravitational wave with a single peak.
The nature of the hydrodynamic impact is fully analogous to processes in fluids under normal terrestrial conditions and is also analogous in principle to cavitation.
After the disappearance of the victim black hole's matter and the filling of its former volume with space and time (increasing noise and a gravitational surge), a decline follows – this is the noise from the final absorption of the victim black hole's remaining matter.
In summary: the gravitational burst profile of a black hole merger appears as "rise – impact – decay".
The result of the merger of two "black hole"-type objects is a readjusted black hole that acted as the absorber.
The total surface area is not of primary importance in stabilizing the dimensions of the resulting black hole.
The new black hole stabilizes and self-regulates solely through its mass and rotation speed. The self-regulation mechanism is diameter variation.

Disruption of an isolated black hole (detection of gravitational waves with two peaks)

An isolated black hole can decay as follows: the event horizon ruptures along the equator upon absorbing a large portion of matter. This results in two hemispheres linked only by mutual gravity.
At the moment the event horizon's integrity is broken, space and time gain the ability to fill the region inside the black hole where space and time are still absent. This filling causes a hydrodynamic impact of space against itself (again, cavitation). This produces the first peak on the gravitational-wave profile.
After the restoration of space-time continuity between the external space and the black hole's interior, the two hemispheres (the two halves of the event horizon) continue to rotate and, under the influence of rotation, tend to flatten into two disks. These two disks inevitably attract each other, resulting in a second gravitational event – a flat explosion and dispersal of the former black hole's matter. This is the second peak on the gravitational-wave profile. The two events in the black hole destruction process follow one another.

    1. Breach of the event horizon with filling of the empty black hole – first peak.
    2. Merger of the hemisphere remnants – second peak.

"Ringdown" – not noise, but dynamic self-tuning of the black hole diameter

The acoustic interpretation of the ripples on the gravitational wave, described as "ringdown," represents an oscillation of the black hole's diameter during self-regulation and stabilization in the process of absorbing external resources.
When the equatorial rotation speed approaches the speed of light, the black hole increases its radius and reduces its rotation speed.
The flat explosion shape and the initial black hole rotation provide an explanation for the flat shape of spiral galaxies.
The discrepancy between the gravitational-wave energy from black hole destruction and calculations based on its mass can be revised by taking into account the energy of the space that impacts itself at the moment of filling the region devoid of "space-time."

Other issues: two Big Bang scenarios, the mass defect of the Universe, 

the shape of the Universe
If the Big Bang occurred as the event of the Universe's origin, and if it was preceded by matter compression, it could have proceeded according to the isolated black hole destruction scenario. During rotation, the most stressed location of the event horizon is the equator. In this case, the Universe would not be perfectly spherical but an oblate spheroid. This explains the non-uniformity of the redshift in the spectra of receding objects.
If the Big Bang did not occur as a prime cause, there exists a second scenario for the Universe's origin. Current observations allow scientists to speak of a uniform expansion of space throughout the observable Universe. Uniform and simultaneous expansion after the Big Bang violates the principle of causality. This can be revised and corrected.
Second scenario for the Universe's origin without a Big Bang:
The pre-existing space for the Universe could have possessed an initial angular momentum, and the possibility of additional external influence that amplified the rotation cannot be ruled out. Under the action of centrifugal forces, the stress at the center of the rotating Universe could have induced a phase transition of space into other forms of energy.
The second scenario for the Universe's origin without a Big Bang but with a phase transition restores causality and leads to the same result as the Big Bang Theory.
The mass defect of the observable Universe (or the energy defect of the Big Bang) should be sought in the primary photon radiation that left the observable region and cooled to approximately 2.7 K at the Universe's periphery, as well as in the total mass of neutrinos that participated in the initial phase of the Big Bang and left the observable region forever.
The question remains open as to whether the cooled primary photons of the Big Bang constitute a sufficiently large mass that the sphere of their current location at the Universe's boundary might one day begin to contract and trigger a new Big Bang.
